{
 "summary": "Findings: five defects require plan changes. The plan introduces a preferences/scheduler feedback loop, leaves delivery-time state stale, duplicates validation without a supported invalid-input producer, changes the form submission contract, and assumes a timer that this project does not contain.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Keep scheduler-derived state out of persisted preferences and remove the feedback loop",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1 and phase-1/t2 (plan.md:38,50)",
   "Failure": "Bidirectional Coupling: the plan makes PreferencesStore call NotificationScheduler, then makes NotificationScheduler call PreferencesStore.update. It adds #rescheduling solely to break this new cycle. Currently the scheduler reads preferences through an injected getter (src/notify/scheduler.ts:28,31,41), and createApp owns composition (src/app.ts:6-10). Parallel State: nextDeliveryAt duplicates a value derived from current preferences and the scheduler's private held queue (src/notify/scheduler.ts:26,32-34,41-43). Putting it in Preferences sends transient queue metadata through persistence and preference listeners (src/preferences/store.ts:18-21). It also permits a saved timestamp to outlive its queue: the plan parses saved nextDeliveryAt, while each new scheduler starts with an empty #held array (src/preferences/store.ts:10-11; src/notify/scheduler.ts:26).",
   "Fix": "Keep the queue and its derived delivery time in the scheduler. Wire preference-change handling from createApp rather than making the store and scheduler depend on each other. Preserve the before-save-completes requirement with an explicitly awaited hook: the existing listener contract returns void and update does not await listeners (src/preferences/store.ts:3,21,24-26). Let the form read a scheduler-owned delivery-time query. Remove the persisted field, parser change, store constructor dependency, attachScheduler cycle, and recursion flag."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Update the delivery-time view on actual queue changes, not only preference saves",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 and phase-2/t2 (plan.md:50,58-60,91,97-98)",
   "Failure": "The only planned nextDeliveryAt writer is reschedule, and the only planned trigger is PreferencesStore.update. However, notify adds held notifications without updating preferences (src/notify/scheduler.ts:30-35). A notification held after startup therefore has no delivery timestamp under the specified flow. Conversely, flush drains the queue without updating preferences (src/notify/scheduler.ts:40-43), leaving a previously recorded timestamp visible after delivery. The existing test directly exercises notify followed by flush (tests/scheduler.test.ts:8-11), so these are existing entry points, not hypothetical callers. The plan's own fields-after-notify and fields-after-delivery expectations are not implemented by its tasks.",
   "Fix": "Derive the displayed delivery time from the current queue and preferences when fields() is read, or explicitly cover every existing queue transition if retaining stored state. Add integration scenarios using createApp: notify then form.fields without a save; flush then form.fields without another save; and save changed quiet hours then form.fields. Assert the specified local HH:MM output, not merely that nextDelivery exists."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Remove unsupported scheduler-side preference repair",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t1 (plan.md:69,79,85-86)",
   "Failure": "Speculative Code: the plan adds missing-field and malformed-time fallbacks inside isQuiet without identifying a supported producer of those values. Preferences requires quietHours (src/preferences/parse.ts:9-12). parsePreferences validates both time components and supplies defaults (src/preferences/parse.ts:19,34-39). Both store construction and every update pass through that parser (src/preferences/store.ts:10-11,18-19), and createApp supplies store.get to the scheduler (src/app.ts:7-8). The other scheduler construction uses DEFAULT_PREFERENCES (tests/scheduler.test.ts:7). The proposed whole-window fallback also differs from the parser's independent start/end defaults. It creates a second validation policy despite the plan's unchanged-parsing constraint (plan.md:19), and its tests require inputs outside the declared Preferences contract.",
   "Fix": "Delete phase-2/t1. Keep validation at parsePreferences. If additional regression coverage is needed, exercise malformed saved/form input through the store and confirm the existing per-field defaults reach the scheduler. Do not manufacture invalid typed scheduler inputs to justify a second validation layer."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Do not make display-only data mandatory on form submission",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t2 (plan.md:91)",
   "Failure": "Adding `nextDelivery: string | undefined` to FormFields makes the property mandatory even when its value is undefined. FormFields is also the input type of SettingsForm.submit (src/ui/settings-form.ts:3-6,17-18). Existing submissions containing only quietStart and quietEnd currently satisfy that public contract; the proposed field would make them fail type checking despite nextDelivery being irrelevant to saving quiet hours. The plan does not acknowledge or justify this input-contract change.",
   "Fix": "Preserve the two-field submission input. Add the delivery value to a separate fields() result type, or make the display property optional if retaining the shared type. Cover submission with only quietStart and quietEnd."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Resolve the nonexistent minute-timer premise",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "context.constraints and phase-1 verification (plan.md:18,64)",
   "Failure": "The plan says the minute timer that calls flush stays, but this directory contains no timer registration. createApp only constructs and returns store, scheduler, and form (src/app.ts:6-10). The scheduler contains a comment claiming a minute-timer caller, not a registration (src/notify/scheduler.ts:39-44). The sole actual flush call in the supplied code is a manual call in tests/scheduler.test.ts:10. Searching all supplied files finds no setInterval or setTimeout. Therefore the plan has not verified the claimed existing delivery driver, and its test commands cannot establish that real-time delivery continues through it.",
   "Fix": "Identify the actual external timer owner and its integration boundary, or explicitly correct the plan to state that this fixture only exposes flush for a caller to drive. Scope verification to the real boundary. Do not silently add a timer and lifecycle machinery as an unrelated implementation change."
  }
 ],
 "verified_context": "Read the complete plan, all five source files, both existing test files, and package.json. The named source files and current methods exist. The parser is shared by saved preferences and form updates; updates persist before invoking listeners. Local-time and overnight semantics are implemented in src/notify/scheduler.ts:17-21. The test command is bun test (package.json:5-6). tests/store.test.ts and tests/settings-form.test.ts are planned new files, not existing coverage. No code was changed or executed; findings concern the specified structure and traced source paths, not unverified runtime races."
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: Four plan defects need correction: duplicated daily aggregation, a forwarding-only exporter class, an incorrect missing-range response contract, and incomplete route verification.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Reuse the daily sales aggregation instead of creating a second owner",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 (plan.md:46)",
   "Failure": "Single Source of Truth: The proposed groupByDay duplicates salesByDay, which already groups by store-local date, sums orders, units and revenue cents, and sorts the rows (src/reports/sales.ts:19-27). HTML already consumes that function (src/reports/sales.ts:30-32). A separate CSV aggregation creates two independent definitions of the same daily report and allows the formats to diverge.",
   "Fix": "Build the CSV rows from salesByDay(sales). Keep only CSV column mapping and cents-to-dollars formatting in salesCsv. Extend tests/sales.test.ts for aggregation assertions, and test the CSV representation separately."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Remove the forwarding-only CsvExporter class and injected parameter",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1, phase-1/t2, phase-2/t1 (plan.md:36-41,46,71)",
   "Failure": "Shallow Wrapper: CsvExporter.export only forwards to toCsv, with the same parameters and return value. CSV encoding already has a single owner in src/format/csv.ts:1-7. The existing report imports that function directly (src/reports/stock.ts:1,8-9), and its route supplies only report data (src/http/report-routes.ts:22-23). The class adds construction, a public parameter and an equivalence test without adding a capability.",
   "Fix": "Have salesCsv(sales) import toCsv directly, following stockCsv. Remove the exporter file, exporter-equivalence test, injected parameter and route construction."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Correct the missing-range test to match the handler's rejection contract",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t1 (plan.md:78)",
   "Failure": "The plan says a missing range 'returns 400, as for the HTML report'. range instead throws a Response with status 400 (src/http/report-routes.ts:9-13), and handleReport does not catch it (src/http/report-routes.ts:16-26). A direct runtime probe confirmed that the HTML handler rejects with a Response, rather than resolving to one. No HTTP server or exception adapter exists in the supplied project to establish an externally returned 400.",
   "Fix": "Specify a handler test that catches the rejected Response and asserts its status, preserving the HTML contract. Do not add CSV-only exception conversion. If an actual HTTP response is intended as acceptance evidence, identify the server adapter before claiming that behavior."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Verify range forwarding, response contents and unchanged HTML behavior",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t1 and phase-2 verification (plan.md:76-83; constraints at plan.md:19-20)",
   "Failure": "The proposed route tests assert only status, headers and missing-range behavior. They do not prove that the selected from/to values reach salesBetween or that its returned sales become the CSV body. The existing HTML route forwards parsed Dates directly (src/http/report-routes.ts:18-20), and ReportSources accepts those Dates (src/http/report-routes.ts:4-6). Existing tests cover only local-day grouping and CSV quoting (tests/sales.test.ts:4-9; tests/csv.test.ts:4-5), so running bun test does not supply the missing route or unchanged-HTML evidence.",
   "Fix": "Extend the planned route test to record and assert both source arguments and the exact CSV body for returned sales. Add an HTML-route regression assertion for the same input and range. Assert that invalid or missing range rejects before calling the source."
  }
 ],
 "reviewed_evidence": "Read the complete plan and every project source and test file. Verified Sale and SalesDay fields, store-local grouping and sorting, HTML formatting, stockCsv's direct toCsv use, CSV escaping and CRLF output, formatDate's signature and construction, America/Chicago settings, route paths, range parsing and source signatures. Verified the test command in package.json:5-6. The proposed new exporter, sales CSV and route-test files do not yet exist. Existing-code claims otherwise match the inspected files, apart from the missing-range return claim. No implementation changes or suite runs were performed."
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: 7 defects. The plan does not update delivery information on queue changes, introduces a persistence feedback loop, assumes a nonexistent timer, duplicates input validation, omits display formatting, and changes the submit contract. Its re-entrancy flag also leaves an unverified concurrent-save failure path.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Delivery information is not updated when notifications enter or leave the queue",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2; phase-2/t2 (plan.md:50,58-59,91,97-98)",
   "Failure": "The plan computes nextDeliveryAt only in reschedule(), which phase-1/t1 invokes from PreferencesStore.update(). However, notify() adds held notifications without updating preferences (src/notify/scheduler.ts:30-35), and flush() removes them without updating preferences (src/notify/scheduler.ts:40-43). Consequently, holding the first notification does not populate the field, and ordinary flushing does not clear a previously populated field. The planned settings tests explicitly require both states.",
   "Fix": "Derive delivery information from the scheduler's current held queue, current preferences, and time. Cover notify -> fields(), quiet-hours save -> fields(), and ordinary flush -> fields(), without inserting an extra preferences save into those tests."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Persisting scheduler-derived state creates an unnecessary feedback loop and stale restored state",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1 and phase-1/t2 (plan.md:38,50)",
   "Failure": "One-Direction Data Flow: the proposed store calls the scheduler, which calls store.update(), requiring a suppression flag solely to break the introduced cycle. PreferencesStore already offers subscriptions (src/preferences/store.ts:24-27), and createApp already owns composition (src/app.ts:6-10).\nThe proposed derived-state update also persists and notifies because every update does both (src/preferences/store.ts:18-21). It turns one preference save into two persistence operations.\nThe queue belongs to each scheduler instance and starts empty (src/notify/scheduler.ts:25-28), whereas saved preferences are restored independently (src/preferences/store.ts:10-11). Passing a saved nextDeliveryAt through parsing therefore allows the form to display a delivery time after recreation when no notification is held.",
   "Fix": "Keep delivery information scheduler-owned and derived, rather than adding it to Preferences or its saved schema. Wire awaited preference-change re-evaluation at the composition boundary, and give the form read access to scheduler delivery information. Existing subscription callbacks are synchronous and are not awaited (src/preferences/store.ts:3,21), so merely adding an async subscriber is not sufficient."
  },
  {
   "Title": "The recursion flag does not distinguish recursive metadata writes from genuine preference saves",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 (plan.md:50,60)",
   "Failure": "UNVERIFIED runtime interleaving: the proposed #rescheduling flag skips every reschedule while set, not just the scheduler's metadata write. Existing update() awaits an injected persistence promise and does not serialize calls (src/preferences/store.ts:10,18-21). Under the proposed algorithm, one reschedule can await its nested metadata persistence while another quiet-hours save completes persistence. The second save then encounters the flag and skips the re-evaluation required before its completion (plan.md:116-117). The planned test covers recursive suppression only, not this distinct save. No implemented flag exists to execute this interleaving against.",
   "Fix": "Remove the feedback write and its flag as part of the ownership correction. If the flag remains, explicitly distinguish internal metadata writes from real preference changes and add a controlled overlapping-persistence test that proves every quiet-hours save re-evaluates before resolving."
  },
  {
   "Title": "The plan assumes a minute timer that the supplied project does not contain",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "phase-1; context.constraints (plan.md:18,26,50)",
   "Failure": "The scheduler comment mentions an app minute timer (src/notify/scheduler.ts:39), but createApp only constructs and returns objects (src/app.ts:6-10). A complete repository search found no timer registration; the only existing flush() invocation is the manually supplied call in tests/scheduler.test.ts:10. Keeping the supposed timer cannot establish delivery at the displayed future time. Calling reschedule() only on preference updates does not supply that missing automatic delivery path.",
   "Fix": "Identify the actual timer owner and include its integration evidence and acceptance check. If this directory is the complete application, the plan must explicitly provide the timed flush path and its lifecycle rather than assume one exists."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Scheduler fallback validation duplicates the established input boundary without a supported producer",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t1 (plan.md:79,85-86)",
   "Failure": "Speculative Code: the plan justifies a second defaulting mechanism with corrupt preferences, but both store construction and update pass through parsePreferences (src/preferences/store.ts:10-11,18-19). That parser supplies quietHours and validates each endpoint against the existing time expression (src/preferences/parse.ts:19,34-39). The app's scheduler reads this normalized store (src/app.ts:8). No inspected production path supplies the missing or malformed quiet hours targeted by these new branches.\nThe proposed whole-window fallback also differs from the parser's independent endpoint defaults (src/preferences/parse.ts:35-36), creating two normalization policies.",
   "Fix": "Remove phase-2/t1 and its invalid-Preferences tests. Keep normalization at parsePreferences. Add another validation boundary only if a concrete supported producer bypassing that boundary is identified."
  },
  {
   "Title": "The display task passes through an ISO timestamp instead of specifying the promised local-time text",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t2 (plan.md:91,97-98); phase-1/t2 (plan.md:50)",
   "Failure": "The plan defines nextDeliveryAt as an ISO timestamp, then says to read it into nextDelivery to show \"Held notifications arrive at HH:MM\". It specifies no local-time conversion, HH:MM formatting, or message construction. SettingsForm.fields() is the entire settings presentation surface in this project and currently returns only the quiet-hour strings (src/ui/settings-form.ts:12-15); no downstream formatter or renderer exists in the supplied files. The proposed tests assert nextDelivery's presence, not the promised display value.",
   "Fix": "Specify the exact output contract for nextDelivery, including local HH:MM formatting and the intended message. Test the exact displayed value, including an overnight delivery, rather than only whether the field exists."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Adding a required display field also changes the form submission input contract",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t2 (plan.md:91)",
   "Failure": "FormFields is both the return type of fields() and the input type of submit() (src/ui/settings-form.ts:3-6,12,17). Adding `nextDelivery: string | undefined` makes that property required even when its value can be undefined. Existing two-property submissions therefore cease to satisfy the public input type, although submit only needs quietStart and quietEnd (src/ui/settings-form.ts:17-18). The plan describes a display addition, not a requirement for callers to submit scheduler metadata.",
   "Fix": "Keep the existing submission shape. Add delivery information to a separate output/view type, or otherwise keep the display property out of the required submit input. Include a submission test using only quietStart and quietEnd."
  }
 ],
 "evidence_reviewed": "Read the complete plan, package.json, every source file, and both existing tests. Verified all named existing surfaces and searched the complete supplied project for timer registrations, flush callers, subscriptions, constructor callers, and form consumers. No files were changed and no tests were run."
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: The plan needs revision before implementation. Its main defect is persisting scheduler-derived state through a circular store/scheduler dependency. It also omits delivery-state updates on normal queue operations, adds unreachable validation, and leaves API, formatting, timer, and concurrency gaps.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Keep delivery metadata with the scheduler instead of persisting it as a preference",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1 and phase-1/t2 (plan.md:38,50)",
   "Failure": "Single Source of Truth: The plan stores a value derived from the scheduler's private queue in persisted preferences. The queue is initialized empty in src/notify/scheduler.ts:25-26, while saved preferences are loaded in src/preferences/store.ts:10-11. Passing nextDeliveryAt through the parser would therefore restore delivery metadata without restoring any corresponding notifications. The proposed store-to-scheduler-to-store write cycle also introduces an attachment method, a second persistence operation, listener notifications, and a recursion flag solely to synchronize this duplicate state. Every store.update persists and notifies at src/preferences/store.ts:18-21. Current composition is already centralized in src/app.ts:6-10, and the scheduler currently depends only on a preference getter at src/notify/scheduler.ts:28.",
   "Fix": "Keep the next-delivery calculation in the scheduler and expose a read-only query to the settings form through createApp. Coordinate an awaited reevaluation after a preference save without writing derived metadata back through store.update. Remove the persisted field, scheduler attachment cycle, and recursion flag. Verify that a newly created app with no held notifications has no delivery time."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Normal enqueue and flush operations never update the proposed delivery field",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 and phase-2/t2 (plan.md:50,58-60,91,97-98)",
   "Failure": "The plan only computes nextDeliveryAt inside reschedule, and only invokes reschedule from PreferencesStore.update. Normal notify pushes onto the queue and returns at src/notify/scheduler.ts:30-35; normal flush removes queued notifications at src/notify/scheduler.ts:40-43. Neither path changes preferences. Consequently, holding a notification without a settings save has no planned path to populate the displayed time, and flushing without another save has no planned path to clear it. The existing delivery test uses exactly notify followed by flush, with no preference update, at tests/scheduler.test.ts:7-11.",
   "Fix": "Make the displayed value derive from the live queue and current preferences, including ordinary notify and flush operations. Test fields() immediately after notify and immediately after flush through createApp, without an intervening settings save or manual reschedule."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Remove downstream validation for states the preference boundary already excludes",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t1 (plan.md:79-86)",
   "Failure": "Speculative Code: The proposed missing-window and malformed-time branches have no supported producer in this project. Preferences requires quietHours at src/preferences/parse.ts:9-12; parsePreferences validates and defaults each endpoint at src/preferences/parse.ts:19,34-39. PreferencesStore parses both saved input and updates at src/preferences/store.ts:10-11,18-19, and createApp supplies that store to the scheduler at src/app.ts:7-8. The existing direct scheduler test supplies valid DEFAULT_PREFERENCES at tests/scheduler.test.ts:3,7. A whole-window fallback also differs from the parser's independent endpoint defaults. The plan discloses no evidence for bypassing this boundary.",
   "Fix": "Delete this hardening task and its invalid-Preferences tests. Keep malformed raw-input coverage at parsePreferences or PreferencesStore, preserving the existing per-endpoint default rules."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Do not make a display-only value mandatory in settings submissions",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t2 (plan.md:91)",
   "Failure": "The plan adds required nextDelivery: string | undefined to FormFields. FormFields currently contains only quietStart and quietEnd at src/ui/settings-form.ts:3-6, and it is also the public submit input at src/ui/settings-form.ts:17-18. The addition makes previously valid two-field submissions invalid at the type level, although delivery text is not submitted or saved. The plan does not analyze or authorize that input-contract change.",
   "Fix": "Keep submit accepting its existing editable fields. Add delivery information only to the returned view shape, or otherwise preserve compatibility for callers that submit only quietStart and quietEnd. Include a type-level check because bun test alone does not establish input compatibility."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Specify and verify local display formatting rather than returning the persisted ISO string",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2/t2 and directive d2 (plan.md:91,97-98,120-122)",
   "Failure": "The plan specifies an ISO timestamp in phase-1/t2, then says to read it into nextDelivery to show \"Held notifications arrive at HH:MM\". It supplies no formatting step, and its checks only assert that nextDelivery exists or is undefined. The complete settings surface is fields() at src/ui/settings-form.ts:12-15; there is no downstream renderer in this project to supply the promised text or local-time conversion. QuietHours explicitly uses local time at src/preferences/parse.ts:3.",
   "Fix": "Specify the exact nextDelivery display contract and format the scheduler's delivery instant into local HH:MM at the view boundary. Assert the exact promised message, including zero-padding and the absence of a message when nothing is held."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Correct the unsupported claim that an app minute timer already exists",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1 verification and context constraint (plan.md:18,64)",
   "Failure": "The plan says the minute timer that calls flush stays. The supplied project has no timer registration. createApp only constructs and returns objects at src/app.ts:6-10. src/notify/scheduler.ts:39 documents an intended minute-timer caller, but the only actual flush call is the manual test call at tests/scheduler.test.ts:10. A comment is not an implementation or an acceptance path. The plan therefore cannot treat automatic end-of-window delivery as an existing verified behavior.",
   "Fix": "Correct the repository-context claim. Identify an external timer owner if one exists and state that boundary explicitly. Otherwise flag the missing timer as a design decision instead of silently assuming or adding it. Keep the planned tests explicit about whether they verify manual flush or actual scheduled delivery."
  },
  {
   "Title": "The recursion flag lacks a proof path for overlapping saves",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2 and directive d1 (plan.md:50,60,115-117)",
   "Failure": "UNVERIFIED: A store-wide #rescheduling flag may skip reevaluation for a distinct save, not just the recursive metadata write. update has an asynchronous persistence boundary at src/preferences/store.ts:18-21, and flush awaits each sender at src/notify/scheduler.ts:40-43. Neither public operation serializes callers. The proposed test only checks that the metadata write does not recurse; it does not establish that every real save reevaluates before resolving. The plan also does not specify flag cleanup after rejection. These are gaps in the proposed mechanism, not observed failures of implemented code.",
   "Fix": "Prefer removing the recursive write and flag as described above. If retained, distinguish internal metadata writes from independent saves, define rejection cleanup, and add a controlled overlapping-save test proving directive d1 for each save."
  }
 ],
 "review_coverage": "Read the complete plan and every project source, test, and package file. Verified preference parsing and ownership, scheduler queue mutations and callers, application composition, settings input/output contracts, existing test coverage, and the absence of timer registration. No files changed and no test suite run. The proposed implementation does not exist, so its concurrency behavior remains explicitly UNVERIFIED."
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: 5 defects. The plan adds an unnecessary cache-migration subsystem, omits dictionary-key safety and duplicate-tag handling, relies on a false sorting premise, and supplies a shell command that discards its query. Review was read-only.",
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Replace the temporary v1 converter with the existing cache-rebuild mechanism",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2, plan.md:50-60",
   "Failure": "Speculative Code: The plan adds a legacy schema, converter, loader branch, tests, and a two-release removal obligation to preserve a disposable cache. The notes explicitly own the data (src/notes/store.ts:20), and incompatible index versions already rebuild and persist through one path (src/search/index-file.ts:4-7,12-21; tests/index-file.test.ts:11-17). The CLI already loads every note before loading the cache (src/cli.ts:9-10). No measured rebuild-cost evidence supports introducing this second construction path. It is not required for tag search or the v2 format.",
   "Fix": "Remove the converter task and its files. Bump INDEX_VERSION, build the tags table in buildIndex, and extend the existing loader test with a matching-fingerprint v1 cache that rebuilds and persists as v2. Retain a migration only if a separate performance requirement and measurements justify it."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Specify own-key-safe tag tables and lookups",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1 and phase-2/t1, plan.md:38,77",
   "Failure": "The plan introduces a string-keyed tag table without accounting for valid tags named '__proto__' or 'constructor'. parseNote accepts both without restriction (src/notes/store.ts:14-17). The existing construction pattern uses an ordinary object and inherited-property-sensitive access (src/search/build.ts:22-25), while search directly converts a property lookup into a Set (src/search/query.ts:7). Bun probes confirmed that applying these patterns to these tag names throws TypeError. JSON-loaded tables also inherit those properties, so safe construction alone does not settle the lookup boundary (src/search/index-file.ts:11,17).",
   "Fix": "Specify own-key-safe construction and lookup for tags, including tables read from JSON. Add build and search coverage for these accepted tag names, an absent inherited-property name, and a persisted v2 reload."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Preserve unique note membership when tags repeat",
   "Severity": "P3",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1-t2, plan.md:38,43-45,50,57-60",
   "Failure": "The plan says to fill postings from note.tags but does not specify per-note deduplication. parseNote preserves duplicates and lowercases before returning them, so 'tags: garden, GARDEN' produces ['garden','garden'] (src/notes/store.ts:15-17), confirmed by a Bun probe. Existing word postings explicitly deduplicate each note's tokens before appending its path (src/search/build.ts:24-25). Directly appending each parsed tag therefore creates duplicate paths and needlessly enlarges the persisted tag table. search's Set conversion would conceal this storage defect (src/search/query.ts:7).",
   "Fix": "Specify one path per note per tag, and test repeated tags with different casing. Apply that invariant to the converter too if it remains."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Correct the claim that index postings are sorted",
   "Severity": "P3",
   "Location": "phase-1/t1; context.patterns[0], plan.md:14",
   "Failure": "The plan claims that index tables contain sorted note paths. buildIndex instead appends paths in caller order and performs no sort (src/search/build.ts:21-28). Only listNotes sorts its input filenames (src/notes/store.ts:22-23), and search independently sorts its final results (src/search/query.ts:8-9). A fresh buildIndex probe with z.md before a.md produced postings ['z.md','a.md']. The stated pattern is not a builder invariant and cannot safely guide the new table or its tests.",
   "Fix": "Correct the context to distinguish caller-ordered postings from sorted search results. Do not introduce posting sorting unless it is a separately required invariant."
  },
  {
